# U-Boot config fragment for the PB2 CAN Carrier image.
# Verify against the pinned U-Boot tree (manifests/sources.lock:
# beagleboard/u-boot @ v2026.01-am62-pocketbeagle2, commit
# 7d2af1199011f2210a862097c78f1b940e5d2631) with scripts/kconfig/
# merge_config.sh before first build.
#
# Real defconfigs confirmed present in the pinned tree (two-stage K3
# boot: R5/SPL then A53/main U-Boot):
#   configs/am62_pocketbeagle2_r5_defconfig
#   configs/am62_pocketbeagle2_a53_defconfig
#
# Confirmed by actually configuring am62_pocketbeagle2_a53_defconfig
# locally: this board uses CONFIG_DM_SERIAL=y with driver-model serial,
# so console selection is DT-driven (via U-Boot's own copy of the board
# device tree, arch/arm/dts/k3-am62-pocketbeagle2-u-boot.dtsi + its
# synced dts/upstream base) -- CONFIG_CONS_INDEX does not apply to this
# target and has been removed from this fragment. CONFIG_BAUDRATE=115200
# and CONFIG_OF_LIBFDT_OVERLAY=y are already the defconfig default.
#
# Uses BeagleBoard's own real, already-signed, unmodified
# bb-u-boot-pocketbeagle2 firmware package rather than a custom U-Boot
# build from this defconfig+fragment (see image-builder/README.md and
# RELEASE_NOTES.md item 1) -- that firmware's own SPL/U-Boot-stage
# console defaults to MAIN_UART6, which is also this carrier's default
# Linux-stage console (work order D-12/§5.5, superseding the earlier
# D-06 UART0-primary decision). Getting SPL/U-Boot output onto UART0
# too would need a real custom-signed U-Boot build (this defconfig +
# fragment + a stdout-path change in k3-am62-pocketbeagle2-u-boot.dtsi,
# then through TI's SYSFW/k3-image-gen signing) -- not done, and out of
# scope now that UART0 is a manual, kernel-stage-only override rather
# than the primary console.
CONFIG_OF_BOARD_FIXUP=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Boot flow -- CONFIRMED by actually building and mounting a real image
# (scripts/assemble-image.sh, see image-builder/README.md for the full
# account). This whole section was corrected against ground truth after
# extracting BeagleBoard's own real, shipped extlinux template
# (bb-u-boot-pocketbeagle2's microsd-extlinux-w-swap.conf) instead of
# guessing from a disabled example comment.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# PB2 images boot via U-Boot distro_bootcmd + extlinux.conf (not the
# legacy uEnv.txt mechanism), read from the FAT boot partition's
# /extlinux/extlinux.conf. Real, confirmed syntax (lowercase, relative to
# the boot-partition root -- NOT the uppercase FDTOVERLAYS /boot/dtbs/
# path this fragment guessed earlier):
#
#   fdtdir /
#   fdtoverlays /overlays/k3-am62-pocketbeagle2-can-carrier.dtbo
#
# scripts/assemble-image.sh injects a new "PB2 CAN Carrier" label with
# this line (set as the default) after setup_sdcard.sh produces the
# image, alongside every stock label (kept intact for fallback/debug) --
# it does not modify image-builder's own templates.
#
# append kernel command line (work order §5.5/D-12), also injected by
# scripts/assemble-image.sh -- CONFIRMED on real hardware (see
# RELEASE_NOTES.md item 6):
#
#   console=ttyS2,115200n8 earlycon=ns16550a,mmio32,0x02860000 systemd.show_status=no
#
# The console= and earlycon= values are the SAME default a stock,
# unmodified PocketBeagle 2 uses -- MAIN_UART6 (ttyS2) as the complete
# console, no carrier-specific console override applied out of the box
# (work order D-12, superseding the earlier D-06 decision to make
# carrier UART0 the default). The carrier overlay still applies UART0's
# pinctrl unconditionally, so the UART0 device always exists as a plain
# serial port; it's just not a registered kernel console by default.
# systemd.show_status=no is the one carrier-specific addition to an
# otherwise-stock command line -- see "UART6/UART0 output split" below
# for why.
#
# MANUAL UART0 OVERRIDE. To switch the kernel-stage console to carrier
# UART0 (P1.30/P1.32), hand-edit the "PB2 CAN Carrier" label's `append`
# line in /extlinux/extlinux.conf on the already-flashed boot partition,
# replacing `console=ttyS2,115200n8` with `console=ttyS7,115200n8` and
# leaving `earlycon=` unchanged:
#
#   console=ttyS7,115200n8 earlycon=ns16550a,mmio32,0x02860000
#
# This is a reboot-time, hand-edited override -- not a separate boot
# label or a build-time image variant. Findings behind these exact
# values, confirmed on real hardware:
# - ttyS7 is UART0's real /dev/ttySN name (confirmed via `dmesg | grep
#   ttyS` on real hardware -- the earlier `ttyS0` guess was wrong; this
#   platform's 8250-omap driver does not call of_alias_get_id(), so
#   /dev/ttySN assignment is registration-order dependent, not DT-alias
#   dependent).
# - earlycon must stay on UART6 (0x02860000) regardless of which
#   console= is active. UART0's peripheral clock is never enabled by the
#   stock, unmodified bb-u-boot-pocketbeagle2 firmware (which only
#   drives MAIN_UART6), so pointing earlycon's raw MMIO access at UART0
#   before Linux's clock driver has run hangs the CPU outright (confirmed
#   on real hardware -- total silence on both UARTs, no USB enumeration).
#   earlycon on UART6 is safe because U-Boot already has that UART
#   clocked and configured.
# - NO baud suffix on earlycon=. Stock BeagleBoard extlinux templates
#   also omit it. Per the kernel's earlycon docs, omitting the baud means
#   "inherit whatever U-Boot already configured" -- appending
#   `,115200n8` makes earlycon actively reprogram the UART against an
#   assumed reference clock, which produced garbled output (confirmed on
#   real hardware).
# - Only ONE console= argument at a time -- never both ttyS7 and ttyS2
#   simultaneously. Registering both as simultaneous kernel consoles was
#   confirmed on real hardware to corrupt UART6's TTY output right at
#   the ttyS7 driver handoff (`printk: legacy console [ttyS7]
#   disabled/enabled`), even though the UART0 peripheral itself is fine
#   in isolation (verified by writing test strings directly to
#   /dev/ttyS7 with `stty`/`printf` while UART6 was still the sole
#   console). Root cause not pinned down further than "dual active
#   kernel consoles on this 8250 setup corrupts one of them during
#   handoff" -- the fix is simply never to enable both. With ttyS7
#   alone, early boot messages (pre ~2.6s, before the 8250 driver takes
#   over from the earlycon boot console) are only visible on UART6's
#   physical pin, not on UART0 -- a documented limitation, same class as
#   the SPL/U-Boot-stage limitation above.
#
# UART6/UART0 OUTPUT SPLIT (default config only -- moot when UART0 is
# manually selected as console= above, since then there is only one
# active UART to begin with). Confirmed on real hardware via a raw byte-
# level UART capture (see tests/): PID1's own direct status writer (the
# "[ OK ] ..." lines) and kernel printk both write to /dev/console
# (bound to ttyS2/UART6) independently and unsynchronized, splicing each
# other's output mid-write on the slow 115200 baud line -- this is what
# produced the garbled/interleaved text and stray BEL bytes observed on
# real hardware, NOT a hardware fault (confirmed reproducible with
# identical timing across boots). systemd.show_status=no stops PID1's
# own status writer from touching /dev/console at all; kernel printk is
# a separate broadcast mechanism and is completely unaffected. Systemd's
# status stream isn't lost -- scripts/assemble-image.sh writes
# /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/pb2-carrier-uart0-forward.conf
# (`ForwardToConsole=yes` + `TTYPath=/dev/ttyS7`) into the rootfs,
# so journald forwards its complete stream (kernel messages plus every
# systemd status event) to UART0 exclusively. This is deliberately NOT
# done via a second kernel console=ttyS7 -- that was confirmed above to
# corrupt UART6 at the ttyS7 driver handoff. journald forwarding is a
# userspace-only mechanism, independent of kernel console registration,
# so it carries none of that risk. Confirmed on real hardware:
# systemd.show_status=no alone -- UART6 stayed completely free of
# "[ OK ]"/interleaving artifacts across a full fresh boot.
#
# ttyS7 has NO login getty -- deliberately. A getty was tried there too
# (sharing the wire with journald's forwarding), and reverted after
# three escalating failures on real hardware:
#  1. serial-getty@.service's stock TTYVHangup=yes performs a
#     vhangup(2) as part of starting up, which severs journald's
#     already-open TTYPath connection the moment the getty starts --
#     UART0 goes silent for the rest of boot. Attempted fix:
#     TTYVHangup=no via a serial-getty@ttyS7.service.d/override.conf
#     drop-in.
#  2. That exposed a second, independent bug: nothing configures
#     ttyS7's baud rate before journald's own early writes (not a
#     kernel console= target, so the kernel's console driver never
#     touches it, and agetty normally would but starts far later than
#     journald: ~25s+ vs ~5-6s). A raw byte-level capture showed a long
#     burst of garbage/NUL bytes until a manual `stty` command fixed
#     it -- both the manual test and journald's subsequent writes then
#     came through clean. Fixed independently of getty via an
#     ExecStartPre= on systemd-journald.service itself
#     (/etc/systemd/system/systemd-journald.service.d/
#     pb2-carrier-ttyS7-baud.conf: `stty -F /dev/ttyS7 115200 raw
#     -echo`), forcing the baud rate before journald's first write.
#     This fix is kept -- it's the only thing that configures ttyS7's
#     line settings now that there's no getty either.
#  3. With both of those in place, agetty on ttyS7 (running with
#     TTYVHangup=no) then failed to terminate cleanly on shutdown --
#     reboot/poweroff stalled for ~25s waiting to "abandon session
#     scope" before giving up. Not acceptable for an unattended
#     deployment.
# Resolved by removing the ttyS7 getty entirely rather than chasing a
# fourth workaround -- ttyS7 is dedicated solely to journald's forwarded
# stream, full session, nothing else ever contends for that tty. Login
# when connected to UART0 is via the USB-C CDC-ACM gadget or SSH
# instead. The baud-rate fix alone (with the since-reverted
# TTYVHangup=no + getty still in place) was confirmed clean on real
# hardware; the final configuration (getty removed) has not yet been
# re-tested on real hardware.
